b/ref 17592186435273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435292 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137154" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435292 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435292 #edb/ref 1071 "$7\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\times 1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435292 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f66c5704-c44a-54d2-8737-7a35f6074418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186435292 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write $7\\,\\frac{1}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n7\\,\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{(7\\times 5)+1}{5}=\\frac{36}{5}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(1\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{5}\\times \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{5}\\times \\frac{5}{4} & =\\frac{36}{4}\\times \\frac{5}{5} \\\\  & =9\\times 1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\times 1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=9\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
